            PHP

    <?php
// PHP program for memoized Fibonacci number

define("MAX", 100);
define("NIL", -1);

// Lookup table for memoization
$lookup = array_fill(0, MAX, NIL);

// Function to initialize the lookup table (optional in PHP)
function initialize() {
    global $lookup;
    for ($i = 0; $i < MAX; $i++) {
        $lookup[$i] = NIL;
    }
}

// Recursive function to find nth Fibonacci number using memoization
function fib($n) {
    global $lookup;

    if ($lookup[$n] === NIL) {
        if ($n <= 1) {
            $lookup[$n] = $n;
        } else {
            $lookup[$n] = fib($n - 1) + fib($n - 2);
        }
    }
    return $lookup[$n];
}

// Driver code
initialize();
$n = 5;
echo "Fibonacci number is " . fib($n);
?>

OutputFibonacci number is 5Time Complexity: O(n). This is because the algorithm computes each Fibonacci number only once and stores the result in an array for future use. Subsequent calls to the function with the same input value of n will retrieve the stored value from the lookup table, avoiding the need to recompute it. Therefore, the time complexity is linear, and the algorithm is very efficient for large values of n.Auxiliary Space: O(n) as lookup table has been created.Tabulation (Bottom Up): The tabulated program for a given problem builds a table in a bottom-up fashion and returns the last entry from the table. For example, for the same Fibonacci number, we first calculate fib(0) then fib(1) then fib(2) then fib(3), and so on. So literally, we are building the solutions to subproblems bottom-up. Following is the tabulated version for the nth Fibonacci Number.
            C++

    /* C++ program for Tabulated version */

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int fib(int n)
{
    int f[n + 1];
    int i;
    f[0] = 0;
    f[1] = 1;
    for (i = 2; i <= n; i++)
        f[i] = f[i - 1] + f[i - 2];

    return f[n];
}

int main()
{
    int n = 5;
    printf("Fibonacci number is %d ", fib(n));
    return 0;
}


